1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,877
Sometimes one file needs two names, or has to appear in two places at once.

2
00:00:04,897 --> 00:00:10,513
Links do that, and there are two kinds that behave very differently when something goes wrong.

3
00:00:10,983 --> 00:00:13,438
In this video: create hard and soft links.

4
00:00:17,458 --> 00:00:20,041
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:20,511 --> 00:00:22,091
Create hard and soft links.

6
00:00:22,561 --> 00:00:26,460
We'll make one file, give it a hard link and a soft link, change it,

7
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:29,687
and then delete the original to see which link survives.

8
00:00:37,207 --> 00:00:41,749
One idea first. A file has two parts: a name, and the data on disk.

9
00:00:41,769 --> 00:00:45,358
Linux keeps the data under a number called the inode.

10
00:00:51,828 --> 00:00:56,522
A hard link is a second name for the same data. A soft link is different.

11
00:00:56,542 --> 00:01:01,237
It is a tiny file that stores a path, like a signpost pointing at a name.

12
00:01:11,757 --> 00:01:16,618
First, a folder and a file to link to. mkdir makes the folder links.

13
00:01:16,638 --> 00:01:22,361
echo with the greater-than sign writes original data into report.txt, as before.

14
00:01:28,831 --> 00:01:31,463
ls -l shows one file, fourteen bytes.

15
00:01:31,483 --> 00:01:36,982
The number after the permissions is the link count. It is 1: one name so far.

16
00:01:41,502 --> 00:01:45,405
ln, short for link, makes a link. The pattern is like cp:

17
00:01:45,425 --> 00:01:50,911
the existing file first, then the new name. With no option, you get a hard link.

18
00:01:50,931 --> 00:01:54,559
report-hard.txt becomes a second name for report.txt.

19
00:01:59,029 --> 00:02:04,157
ls -li. The -i option adds the inode number at the start of each line.

20
00:02:04,177 --> 00:02:10,188
Both names show the same inode, and the link count is now 2. Same data, two names.

21
00:02:16,658 --> 00:02:18,857
cat on the hard link prints the same text.

22
00:02:22,877 --> 00:02:28,236
ln -s makes a soft link, also called a symbolic link. The order is the same:

23
00:02:28,256 --> 00:02:30,572
target first, then the link name.

24
00:02:30,592 --> 00:02:36,446
The target is written relative to the folder the link lives in, so just report.txt.

25
00:02:40,916 --> 00:02:46,388
ls -li again. The soft link has its own inode, and a type letter l, for link.

26
00:02:46,408 --> 00:02:48,600
An arrow shows where it points.

27
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:52,023
Its link count stays 1, and its size, ten bytes,

28
00:02:52,043 --> 00:02:55,019
is just the length of the text report.txt.

29
00:03:01,489 --> 00:03:04,608
cat follows the signpost and prints the data.

30
00:03:04,628 --> 00:03:08,445
readlink prints what a soft link points to: report.txt.

31
00:03:14,965 --> 00:03:20,413
Now we change the file through one name. echo with two greater-than signs appends:

32
00:03:20,433 --> 00:03:25,548
it adds a line instead of replacing the file. We add more data to report.txt.

33
00:03:29,018 --> 00:03:30,640
Both links show the new line.

34
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:35,056
The hard link shares the data, and the soft link follows the name to the data.

35
00:03:40,576 --> 00:03:45,420
Now the real test. rm removes report.txt, the original name.

36
00:03:48,390 --> 00:03:51,842
ls -li. The hard link is still here, same inode,

37
00:03:51,862 --> 00:03:54,663
and its link count dropped from 2 to 1.

38
00:03:54,683 --> 00:03:58,930
The data was never deleted, because one name still uses it.

39
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:05,243
cat on the hard link still prints both lines.

40
00:04:05,263 --> 00:04:09,187
The soft link, though, points at a name that no longer exists,

41
00:04:09,207 --> 00:04:13,132
and cat says no such file or directory. That is a broken link.

42
00:04:18,602 --> 00:04:22,839
Remember: a hard link survives the original. A soft link breaks.

43
00:04:22,859 --> 00:04:26,896
A hard link cannot cross filesystems or point to a directory,

44
00:04:26,916 --> 00:04:28,758
but a soft link can do both.

45
00:04:33,278 --> 00:04:36,714
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

46
00:04:38,234 --> 00:04:40,960
Links live on disk, so they survive a reboot.

47
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:43,462
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

48
00:04:46,932 --> 00:04:48,727
Same inodes, same link counts.

49
00:04:48,747 --> 00:04:53,263
The hard link still has its data, and the broken soft link is still broken,

50
00:04:53,283 --> 00:04:56,469
while the soft link we made for practice still works.

51
00:05:08,989 --> 00:05:14,191
Your turn. In the links folder, create base.txt holding the word lab9.

52
00:05:14,211 --> 00:05:20,233
Then make base-hard.txt, a hard link to it, and base-soft.txt, a soft link to it.

53
00:05:24,703 --> 00:05:28,016
Check with ls -li: the hard link shares the inode.

54
00:05:28,036 --> 00:05:31,616
Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

55
00:05:38,136 --> 00:05:43,513
Next up: List, set, and change standard u g o slash r w x permissions.

56
00:05:43,983 --> 00:05:46,886
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
